-- A fail test: this file must NOT compile. `or` supplies a default for a
-- nullable left operand, and only nil takes the fallback. For every other
-- inner type that reads the same as Lua's truthiness `or`, because Lua has no
-- other falsy value a `T?` can hold. `bool?` is the exception: Lua would fall
-- through on a present `false` and this rule would not, so a reader cannot
-- tell which one a `bool?` left operand means. `stored == true` already spells
-- "set and true" -- see nullable_or_default.walu.
-- conformance: error=whether a present 'false' takes the fallback is ambiguous
-- conformance: error=Write 'flag == true' for set-and-true
local stored: bool? = false
local enabled: bool = stored or true
assert(enabled == false)
